s of the listed tiles, and I have 11, but for gameplay, I need to discard one from the valid actions.\\n\\nBut valid actions are 9 options, all singles.\\n\\nPerhaps the system means I have at least one of each, and I can discard any.\\n\\nFocus on strategy.\\n\\nKey observations:\\n\\n- I have [T7,T8,T9] \\u2014 a complete Chow.\\n- I have [T1,T2,T2,T3] \\u2014 can form [T1,T2,T3] and have a T2 pair.\\n- So two Chows in Bamboos almost set.\\n- Third meld is [W5,W6,W7] \\u2014 Characters.\\n- Remaining: B4, B5, F4, J1 \\u2014 all singletons.\\n\\nIf I can form a fourth meld and a pair, I can win.\\n\\nBest pair candidates: T2 (already two), or make a pair from B4/B5/F4/J1.\\n\\nBut I have only one each.\\n\\nSo I need to draw or claim to make a pair.\\n\\nHonors F4 and J1 are weak; B4 and B5 are middle dots.\\n\\nB4 and B5 can form [B4,B5,B6] but B6 was discarded by previous player \\u2014 so very dangerous to try, as B6 is out.\\n\\nAlso, B6 is now known to be discarded, so cannot be drawn.\\n\\nSo [B4,B5,B6] is impossible.\\n\\nOther possibilities: [B3,B4,B5] \\u2014 need B3; or [B4,B5,B6] dead.\\n\\nSo B4 and B5 together give only inside wait for B3 or B6 \\u2014 B6 dead, B3 possible.\\n\\nBut low probability.\\n\\nBetter to discard one of the isolated suited tiles or honors.\\n\\nNow, patterns:\\n\\n- All Chows (2 points): if I have four chows and a pair, all concealed or mixed.\\n- Half Flush: if I can get rid of one honor, but I have two.\\n- Mixed Straight (8 points): three chows 123, 456, 789 in different suits.\\n\\nI have:\\n- [T1,T2,T3] (if formed)\\n- [T7,T8,T9]\\n- [W5,W6,W7] \\u2014 this is 567 in Characters\\n\\nSo if I can treat [W5,W6,W7] as the 567, then I have 123 and 789 in T, and 567 in W \\u2014 perfect for Mixed Straight (8 points).\\n\\nYes! This is promising.\\n\\nMixed Straight requires three chows: 123, 456, 789 in different suits.\\n\\nHere:\\n- T: [T1,T2,T3] and [T7,T8,T9] \\u2014 two in same suit.\\n- But I can use only one per suit.\\n\\nSo I can use either [T1,T2,T3] or [T7,T8,T9], not both.\\n\\nAnd [W5,W6,W7] is 567.\\n\\nSo I need a 456 in another suit.\\n\\nI have B4 and B5 \\u2014 if I can get B6, I\\u2019d have [B4,B5,B6], but B6 is discarded \\u2014 impossible.\\n\\n[T4,T5,T6] \\u2014 do I have any? No T4,T5,T6.\\n\\nSo cannot form 456.\\n\\nThus, Mixed Straight not possible.\\n\\nPure Shifted Chows? Unlikely.\\n\\nBack to hand.\\n\\nI have two potential chows in T: [T1,T2,T3] and [T7,T8,T9].\\n\\nIf I keep both, I can have two chows in same suit.\\n\\nBut I need two more melds.\\n\\nCurrently have [W5,W6,W7] as one.\\n\\nNeed another meld and a pair.\\n\\nBest bet: aim for All Chows (2 points) + possibly Mixed Double Chow or Short Straight.\\n\\nFor example, if I have [T1,T2,T3], [T7,T8,T9], [W5,W6,W7], and then another chow from B4,B5 + draw B3 or B6, but B6 out.\\n\\nOr make a pair.\\n\\nThe safest discard is one of the honors or a less useful tile.\\n\\nOptions:\\n- F4: North Wind, not prevalent or seat wind (both are F2=South), so no value.\\n- J1: Red Dragon, could be part of Dragon Pung (2 points), but only one.\\n- B4, B5: part of possible chow, but B6 gone.\\n- T1: part of [T1,T2,T3], but I have T2\\u00d72, so even if I discard T1, I can still use T2,T3 + T4? No, T1,T2,T3 needs T1.\\n- If I keep T2,T2,T3, I can only make [T2,T3,T4] if I draw T4, but not [T1,T2,T3] without T1.\\n\\nSo if I discard T1, I lose [T1,T2,T3], but can keep T2,T2,T3 for [T2,T3,T4] if I draw T4.\\n\\nSimilarly, T3 discard loses both.\\n\\nT2 discard: I have two, so discard one, keep one T2.\\n\\nThen I can still make [T1,T2,T3] or [T2,T3,T4].\\n\\nT7, T8, T9: all needed for [T7,T8,T9], so don't discard any.\\n\\nB4, B5: marginal.\\n\\nF4, J1: weak honors.\\n\\nBest discard candidates: F4 or J1, as they are least connected.\\n\\nBetween them, F4 is wind not matching seat/prevalent, J1 is dragon \\u2014 Dragon Pung is worth 2 points, so better to keep J1 than F4.\\n\\nF4 is completely useless.\\n\\nB4 and B5