revalent Wind or Seat Wind. Risky.\n- **Play J1**: Red Dragon; honorable tile, often collected. 3 copies still unshown, so possible others are using it. But Dragons are commonly targeted, so discard carries risk.\n- **Play T7**: I already have [T6,T7,T8] melded, so holding T7 in hand seems redundant. But since I already used T7 in a meld, and T8 is gone (all discarded or used), T7 might be less useful. However, discarding T7 could feed a chow like [T5,T6,T7] or [T7,T8,T9]. But T8 is exhausted (0 left), so [T7,T8,T9] impossible. Only possible chow with T7 is [T5,T6,T7], but T6 is low in supply (only 2 left, possibly used). So T7 is relatively safe.\n\nReason: Among the options, **Play B3** is the safest discard because:\n- B3 is almost out of circulation (only one unshown, and I hold it).\n- Previous player used B3 in a chow, so they already have one; unlikely to need a second unless going for a pung, but no sign of that.\n- No one has declared a pung of B3.\n- Compared to F2 (seat & prevalent wind) and J1 (dragon), B3 poses minimal risk.\n- Discarding F2 or J1 risks giving opponents valuable honor tiles for scoring patterns.\n\nAnswer: Play B3"}
